# SQL Rules

### The complete set
You can think of the rules like this:
```
| Rule | Parent deleted | What happens to the child row |
|---|---|---|
| `CASCADE` | Yes | Child row is automatically deleted |
| `RESTRICT` | No | Parent deletion is blocked while child rows exist |
| `NO ACTION` | No | Foreign-key constraint prevents invalid deletion |
| `SET NULL` | Yes | Child's foreign-key column is set to `NULL` |
| `SET DEFAULT` | Yes | Child's foreign-key column is set to its default value |
| No rule specified | Usually no | Parent deletion is normally blocked if children exist |
| `ON UPDATE CASCADE` | Parent key changes | Child's foreign-key value is automatically updated |
```

### CASCADE vs SET NULL vs RESTRICT
This is the most important distinction to understand:
                 Parent deleted
                       │
          ┌────────────┼────────────┐
          ↓            ↓            ↓
       CASCADE       SET NULL     RESTRICT
          │            │            │
          ↓            ↓            ↓
   Delete child    Keep child    Keep child
                  FK = NULL     Delete blocked


## SQLAlchemy ORM
Foreign Key Rules — SQLAlchemy ORM

Use ForeignKey() to define database-level foreign-key rules in SQLAlchemy.

ForeignKey(
    "USERS.ID",
    ondelete="CASCADE",
    onupdate="CASCADE"
)

Rule	Meaning
ondelete="CASCADE"	Delete child rows when the parent row is deleted.
ondelete="RESTRICT"	Prevent deleting the parent while child rows exist.
ondelete="NO ACTION"	Do not automatically modify child rows; enforce the FK constraint.
ondelete="SET NULL"	Set the child FK column to NULL when the parent is deleted.
ondelete="SET DEFAULT"	Set the child FK column to its default value when the parent is deleted.
onupdate="CASCADE"	Update child FK values when the referenced parent key changes.
No rule	Use the database's default FK behavior.

Example:

USER_ID: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(
    Integer,
    ForeignKey("USERS.ID", ondelete="CASCADE"),
    nullable=False
)

## MySQL .sql
Foreign Key Rules — MySQL

Use FOREIGN KEY with ON DELETE and ON UPDATE to define database-level rules.

FOREIGN KEY (USER_ID)
    REFERENCES USERS(ID)
    ON DELETE CASCADE
    ON UPDATE CASCADE;

Rule	Meaning
ON DELETE CASCADE	Delete child rows when the parent row is deleted.
ON DELETE RESTRICT	Prevent deleting the parent while child rows exist.
ON DELETE NO ACTION	Do not automatically modify child rows; enforce the FK constraint.
ON DELETE SET NULL	Set the child FK column to NULL when the parent is deleted.
ON DELETE SET DEFAULT	Set the child FK column to its default value when the parent is deleted.
ON UPDATE CASCADE	Update child FK values when the referenced parent key changes.
No rule	Use MySQL's default FK behavior.

Example:

CREATE TABLE ARTIST_PROFILES (
    ID INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
    USER_ID INT NOT NULL,

    CONSTRAINT FK_ARTIST_USER
        FOREIGN KEY (USER_ID)
        REFERENCES USERS(ID)
        ON DELETE CASCADE
        ON UPDATE CASCADE
);
